course: dictionary
lesson: 9707
title: Sulphides: sulphur joined together
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A sulphide is a chemical made when sulphur joins with another element, often a metal. The word sulphur in the name tells you that sulphur is one part of it. «Mixed iron filings and sulphur powder can be separated with a magnet; after a teacher heats them, they form iron sulphide, which the magnet no longer picks out.» Here two things join to make a new substance with different properties.
explain: Hydrogen sulphide is a special sulphide that is a gas. «Rotten eggs smell because bacteria break down proteins with sulphur in them and give off hydrogen sulphide gas.» That is why rotten eggs smell so bad, and noses can sense even tiny amounts of it.
explain: Sulphides can be found in rocks and mud. «In airless marsh mud, bacteria rot dead plants and make methane and smelly hydrogen sulphide, the rotten-egg smell.» Scientists use clues like this to understand where a smell comes from, even when they cannot see the gas itself.
fact: A sulphide is a chemical compound made of sulphur joined with another element, often a metal.
fact: Hydrogen sulphide is a gas that smells of rotten eggs.
fact: Iron sulphide is also called fool's gold, or pyrite.
fact: Some sulphides, such as lead sulphide (galena), are important ores that metals are taken from.
fact: In American spelling, the word is written sulfide.
exercise: What is a sulphide? | *A chemical made of sulphur joined with another element, often a metal | A metal that has been heated until it melts | A gas that smells sweet like flowers | A magnet that picks up iron
exercise: What smell does hydrogen sulphide give off? | Salty, like the sea | Burnt toast | Sweet, like flowers | *Rotten eggs
exercise: «...after a teacher heats them, they form iron sulphide, which the magnet no longer picks out.» What does the magnet do with iron sulphide? | *It no longer picks it up | It makes it glow | It picks it up strongly | It melts it into a liquid
test: Which of these is a sulphide? | *Lead and sulphur joined together | Iron filings on their own | Water and salt mixed in a glass | Sulphur and oxygen with nothing else
test: Fool's gold is a name for which sulphide? | Lead oxide | Hydrogen sulphide | *Iron sulphide | Zinc sulphide
exam: In the marsh sentence, what makes the rotten-egg smell? | Will-o'-the-wisp lights over the water | Dead plants rotting in open air | *Hydrogen sulphide made by bacteria in airless mud | Methane gas on its own
